issue.evidence:
Haiku, AI ban − AI lab (paired, n = 20, my sign-flip tests):
- issues listed 34.2 vs 30.7 (+3.55, p = 0.035);
- characters +1,366 (p = 0.002);
- output tokens +1,766 (p = 0.010);
- recall +5 pp (p = 0.011);
- thinking: log_think (loop) C2 +0.198 (p = 0.019), interaction C3 +0.203 [0.022, 0.390] (p = 0.05), from analysis/secondary_contrasts.csv.

Haiku, mining ban − mining company: issues −0.4 (p = 0.84); characters +607 (p = 0.19).

Pooled 'loop log(n issues listed)' C2 = −0.026 [−0.095, 0.042], p = 0.47. Per model, C2 on issue count:
- Sonnet: p = 0.016, longer for the lab;
- Opus: p = 0.22;
- Fable: p = 0.13;
- Haiku: p = 0.034, shorter for the lab.

Haiku is also the source of the only explicit withholding (ai_industry haiku r07) and of the entire I6 gap.

Caveat: Haiku's AI-lab reviews are about the same as its AI-newsroom ones (30.9 issues, 8,429 characters). The pattern may be 'extra effort for the AI-ban side' rather than 'less for the lab'. Either way, the README's description is wrong for Haiku.
